# Automation and script editing

Keep editing, saving, enabling and running separate. An editor mixes configuration with actions that can affect the home, so people need a readable structure, a draft that stays put and a clear statement of what a test will run. The native editor stays responsible for editing, validation, undo and traces.

This page is contract HA-CTRL-009. Its examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Operation | What to show |
| --- | --- |
| Edit a draft | Unsaved changes. The native editor's state and undo stay available. |
| Save | Say the configuration was saved only after Home Assistant confirms it. Do not say devices changed. |
| Enable | Future matching triggers can run the automation. It does not show that it is running now. |
| Run or test | Say which actions, conditions or branch the chosen native test evaluates, and which targets it can affect. |
| Disable or delete | Explain the effect on future triggers and on a run in progress, as Home Assistant describes it. Do not promise a rollback. |
| Trace | Evidence about one run, with its time and the path it took. It is not proof the automation always works. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Keep schema errors, permission failures, save conflicts and action failures apart. For a field or YAML error, give the exact location and keep the person's text where the editor allows. A lost connection must not read “Saved”. Keep the draft and the native undo if they really are available, and say plainly what cannot be recovered.

Show the hierarchy of nested Choose, If, Repeat and parallel actions. The order can change what runs, so a visual rearrangement must not flatten branches. Keep the native way to move an action without dragging, where one exists. If the installed editor cannot reorder by keyboard, record that as an accessibility gap.

An automation can be saved and disabled, enabled but never triggered, or running now. Do not merge these into one “Complete” state. A manual test can behave differently from the trigger path. Its result does not show that every condition or trigger works.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Use the native labels of the version you document. Before a test, explain the targets it needs, the modes and any significant effects. Keep YAML syntax and entity IDs exact and in a monospaced style. Translating an explanatory label must not change a code value. Give the cause the error supplies separately from any suggestion. Do not blame a device without trace evidence.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Use real headings and nested lists for an outline. In the editor, keep labelled action blocks, keyboard selection, focus after a move or delete, and error navigation that assistive technology can use. Dense code keeps its alignment and native scaling, so do not force the 19px page text into the code editor. Keep Run visually and semantically separate from Save.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-009-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Send people to the native visual or YAML editor under Settings > Automations & scenes. Theme variables do not give an editor an edit model, an undo stack or a trace engine. These fixed forms show content and error placement only. A future scoped adapter must leave the native unsaved-changes state, validation, ordering and callbacks alone, and must record the frontend version it was tested on.

